","scheduling_explanation":"Only sck_pulse drives SCK after the single time-zero SCK = 1'b0 in initialize_interface. Each pulse has a 10 us low phase, a rise at which total_pulses increments once, a sample of MISO/BUSY 1 ns later, and the remaining 9,999 ns high before the fall. The 1 ns delay lets the DUT's posedge nonblocking updates, its registered MISO output, and the combinational BUSY logic settle before sampling. All 32 samples are kept MSB-first, so no serial bit is dropped or inserted. MOSI, RST_N, NORM_CS_N, START and ENC_DEC change only while SCK is low, at or just after the falling edge, which leaves nearly 10 us of setup before the next rising edge. MOSI holds its last bit between transfers. The 1 ns controller-latch wait and the 1 ns post-START settling wait are deducted from the next low phase through a credit counter. This keeps every period at exactly 20 us and adds no clocks. A high BUSY sample on the START edge is not treated as acceptance. Acceptance requires known-high BUSY after START is released, then BUSY high on post-START edge 1, before any later low sample can count as completion. Unknown BUSY on any sampled edge fails. Unknown MISO fails only when a transfer consumes it; samples during reset and processing are ignored. Every loop is bounded: pulse_limit is restricted to 1..64, and transfers and resets use fixed pulse counts. A 100 ms watchdog covers the worst case of 180+66*64 = 4404 pulses, about 88 ms."}
